examples.question_text:
The graph shows $y=x+2$ and $y=-x+4$. Find their point of intersection and verify that it satisfies both equations.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=10mm,gridgrey,thin] (-2,-2) grid (5,5);
\draw[<->,black] (-2.2,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-2.2)--(0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5}{\draw[black] (\v,0.06)--(\v,-0.06) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};\draw[black] (0.06,\v)--(-0.06,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick,<->] (-2,0)--(3,5);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed,<->] (-1,5)--(5,-1);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Read the crossing: $x=1$ and $y=3$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=10mm,gridgrey,thin] (-2,-2) grid (5,5);
\draw[<->,black] (-2.2,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-2.2)--(0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5}{\draw[black] (\v,0.06)--(\v,-0.06) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};\draw[black] (0.06,\v)--(-0.06,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick,<->] (-2,0)--(3,5);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed,<->] (-1,5)--(5,-1);
\draw[black,densely dotted] (1,0)--(1,3)--(0,3);
\fill[black] (1,3) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=west] (p) at (2.4,3.6) {$(1,3)$};
\draw[black,thin,shorten >=2pt] (p.west)--(1,3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $y=x+2$: $1+2=3$.

For $y=-x+4$: $-1+4=3$.

Both equations give $y=3$, so the simultaneous solution is $(1,3)$.

Construct the tables.

$y=2x-2$

| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| $y$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |

$y=4-x$

| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| $y$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ |

Plot the points and join each set with a straight line.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=10mm,gridgrey,thin] (-1,-3) grid (6,5);
\draw[<->,black] (-1.2,0)--(6.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-3.2)--(0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-1,1,2,3,4,5,6}{\draw[black] (\v,0.06)--(\v,-0.06) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5}{\draw[black] (0.06,\v)--(-0.06,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick,<->] (-0.5,-3)--(3.5,5);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed,<->] (-1,5)--(6,-2);
\foreach \p in {(0,-2),(1,0),(2,2),(3,4)}{\fill[seriesblue] \p circle (1.5pt);}
\foreach \p in {(0,4),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)}{\fill[seriesred] \p circle (1.5pt);}
\fill[black] (2,2) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=west] (p) at (3.5,2.5) {$(2,2)$};
\draw[black,thin,shorten >=2pt] (p.west)--(2,2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The lines cross at $(2,2)$.

For $y=2x-2$: $2(2)-2=2$.

For $y=4-x$: $4-2=2$.

The simultaneous solution is $x=2$, $y=2$.

examples.question_text:
The graph shows $A:y=2x+1$, $B:y=2x-3$ and $C:y=-x+3$.

(a) Find and verify the point of intersection of $B$ and $C$.

(b) Do $A$ and $B$ have a point of intersection? Explain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{seriesgreen}{HTML}{4F9B63}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=10mm,gridgrey,thin] (-2,-4) grid (5,6);
\draw[<->,black] (-2.2,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-4.2)--(0,6.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5}{\draw[black] (\v,0.06)--(\v,-0.06) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6}{\draw[black] (0.06,\v)--(-0.06,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick,<->] (-2,-3)--(2.5,6);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed,<->] (-0.5,-4)--(4.5,6);
\draw[seriesgreen,thick,dotted,<->] (-2,5)--(5,-2);
\node[text=seriesblue,anchor=east] at (2.2,5.7) {$A$};
\node[text=seriesred,anchor=west] at (4.5,5.5) {$B$};
\node[text=seriesgreen,anchor=south] at (4.6,-1.6) {$C$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
(a) Follow lines $B$ and $C$ to their crossing: $(2,1)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{seriesgreen}{HTML}{4F9B63}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=10mm,gridgrey,thin] (-2,-4) grid (5,6);
\draw[<->,black] (-2.2,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-4.2)--(0,6.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5}{\draw[black] (\v,0.06)--(\v,-0.06) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6}{\draw[black] (0.06,\v)--(-0.06,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick,<->] (-2,-3)--(2.5,6);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed,<->] (-0.5,-4)--(4.5,6);
\draw[seriesgreen,thick,dotted,<->] (-2,5)--(5,-2);
\node[text=seriesblue,anchor=east] at (2.2,5.7) {$A$};
\node[text=seriesred,anchor=west] at (4.5,5.5) {$B$};
\node[text=seriesgreen,anchor=south] at (4.6,-1.6) {$C$};
\fill[black] (2,1) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=west] (p) at (3.2,2.4) {$(2,1)$};
\draw[black,thin,shorten >=2pt] (p.west)--(2,1);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $B$: $2(2)-3=1$.

For $C$: $-2+3=1$.

The simultaneous solution for $B$ and $C$ is $x=2$, $y=1$.

(b) $A$ and $B$ have the same gradient, $2$, and different $y$-intercepts, $1$ and $-3$. They are parallel, so they have no point of intersection.

examples.question_text:
A learner says $y=x+4$ and $y=-x+2$ intersect at $(2,6)$ because this point lies on $y=x+4$. Use the graph and both equations to assess the claim and give the correct intersection.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=10mm,gridgrey,thin] (-3,-2) grid (4,7);
\draw[<->,black] (-3.2,0)--(4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-2.2)--(0,7.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4}{\draw[black] (\v,0.06)--(\v,-0.06) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7}{\draw[black] (0.06,\v)--(-0.06,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick,<->] (-3,1)--(3,7);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed,<->] (-3,5)--(4,-2);
\draw[black] (1.92,5.92)--(2.08,6.08) (1.92,6.08)--(2.08,5.92);
\node[anchor=west] (p) at (2.7,5.3) {$(2,6)$};
\draw[black,thin,shorten >=2pt] (p.west)--(2,6);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Check the claimed point in both equations.

For $y=x+4$: $2+4=6$.

For $y=-x+2$: $-2+2=0\ne6$.

The point lies on only one line, so the claim is incorrect. Read the actual crossing at $(-1,3)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=10mm,gridgrey,thin] (-3,-2) grid (4,7);
\draw[<->,black] (-3.2,0)--(4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-2.2)--(0,7.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4}{\draw[black] (\v,0.06)--(\v,-0.06) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7}{\draw[black] (0.06,\v)--(-0.06,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick,<->] (-3,1)--(3,7);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed,<->] (-3,5)--(4,-2);
\fill[black] (-1,3) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=west] (p) at (1.2,3.8) {$(-1,3)$};
\draw[black,thin,shorten >=2pt] (p.west)--(-1,3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $y=x+4$: $-1+4=3$.

For $y=-x+2$: $-(-1)+2=3$.

The correct intersection is $(-1,3)$.

examples.question_text:
Find the point of intersection of $y=4x$ and the horizontal line $y=6$. Read it from the graph, then confirm the coordinates by substitution.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=20mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[xstep=10mm,ystep=7mm,gridgrey,thin] (-0.5,-2) grid (3,10);
\draw[<->,black] (-0.6,0)--(3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-2.3)--(0,10.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {0.5,1,1.5,2,2.5,3}{\draw[black] (\v,0.1)--(\v,-0.1) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {-2,2,4,6,8,10}{\draw[black] (0.04,\v)--(-0.04,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick,<->] (-0.5,-2)--(2.5,10);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed,<->] (-0.5,6)--(3,6);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The horizontal line has $y=6$. Its crossing with $y=4x$ is halfway between $x=1$ and $x=2$, at $(1.5,6)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=20mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[xstep=10mm,ystep=7mm,gridgrey,thin] (-0.5,-2) grid (3,10);
\draw[<->,black] (-0.6,0)--(3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-2.3)--(0,10.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {0.5,1,1.5,2,2.5,3}{\draw[black] (\v,0.1)--(\v,-0.1) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {-2,2,4,6,8,10}{\draw[black] (0.04,\v)--(-0.04,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick,<->] (-0.5,-2)--(2.5,10);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed,<->] (-0.5,6)--(3,6);
\draw[black,densely dotted] (1.5,0)--(1.5,6);
\fill[black] (1.5,6) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=west] (p) at (2,4.5) {$(1.5,6)$};
\draw[black,thin,shorten >=2pt] (p.west)--(1.5,6);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Substitute $y=6$ into $y=4x$:

$\begin{aligned}6&=4x\\x&=\frac{6}{4}\\x&=1.5\end{aligned}$

Check: $4(1.5)=6$. The point also lies on $y=6$, so the intersection is $(1.5,6)$.
